  "worked_solution": "Since the point $P$ lies on the unit circle, it must satisfy the Pythagorean theorem: \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y=-\\frac{6}{7}$ into the above and solving for $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{(-\\frac{6}{7})}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+(\\frac{36}{49}) & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =1-\\frac{36}{49} \\\\ {x}^{2} & =\\frac{13}{49} \\\\ x & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{13}{49}} \\\\ x & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we select the positive answer since $x$ is positive in the fourth quadrant. So our final answer is $x=\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{7}.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
